<</E(  join\(\) uses O\(N\) memory for the final string and usually needs the parts collection as well. StringIO uses a growable in- memory buffer and O\(N\) output memory, but avoids repeatedly constructing immutable intermediate strings. Direct file streaming uses O\(1\) extra application memory \(apart from buffering\) because it writes each line and does not retain the complete statement in RAM; it is best when the statement can be consumed as a file. All three efficient methods take O\(N\) time in the total number of characters written. )/K[ 12]/P 124 0 R /Pg 20 0 R /S/H3/T(  join\(\) uses O\(N\) memory for the final string and usually needs the parts collection as well. StringIO uses a growable in- memory buffer and O\(N\) output memory, but avoids repeatedly constructing immutable intermediate strings. Direct file streaming uses O\(1\) extra application memory \(apart from buffering\) because it writes each line and does not retain the complete statement in RAM; it is best when the statement can be consumed as a file. All three efficient methods take O\(N\) time in the total number of characters written. )/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
